First, we realize that $\sin^7(x)$ is just shorthand for $(\sin(x))^7$, which is clearly composite:

$\underbrace{(~\overbrace{\sin(x)}^{\text{inner}}~)^7}_{\text{outer}}$

So if $\sin^7(x)=w(u(x))$, then:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{u(x)}&=\goldD{\sin(x)} &&\text{inner function}
\\\\
w(x)&=x^7&&\text{outer function}
\end{align}$

Before applying the chain rule, let's find the derivatives of the inner and outer functions (work not shown, but hopefully these derivatives are familiar by now).

$\begin{align}
\maroonD{u'(x)}&=\maroonD{\cos(x)}
\\\\
\blueD{w'(x)}&=\blueD{7x^6}
\end{align}$

Now let's apply the chain rule:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}[\sin^7(x)]&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[w\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]
\\\\
&=\blueD{w'\Bigl(\goldD{u(x)}\Bigr)}\cdot\maroonD{u'(x)}
\\\\
&=\blueD{7(\goldD{\sin(x)})^6} \cdot \maroonD{\cos(x)}
\\\\
&=7\sin^6(x)\cos(x)
\end{align}$
